{
  "title": "Base of Thumb Fractures",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/hand/6036/base-of-thumb-fractures",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Jun 2 2025",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Base of Thumb metacarpal fractures can be extra-articular fractures, Bennett fractures (partial intra-articular), or Rolando fractures (complete intra-articular).",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis can be made by orthogonal radiographs of the thumb.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment ranges from splint immobilization for certain extra-articular fractures to surgical fixation for displaced Bennett or Rolando fractures.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Incidence",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "80% of thumb fractures involve the metacarpal base",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**the most common pattern is extraarticular epibasal fracture**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Etiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Pathophysiology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "mechanism of injury",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "most Bennett and Rolando are fractures caused by axial force applied to the thumb in flexion",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "pathoanatomy",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "imperfect reductions and above forces lead to increased joint contact pressures and subsequent predisposition to early arthritis",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "3",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**excessive angulation may lead to MCP joint hyperextension deformity**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Anatomy",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Osteology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "CMC joint is a saddle-shaped joint composed of the trapezium and the base of the thumb (1st) metacarpal",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "flexion-extension motion",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6036/images/saddle_shaped_joint.jpg",
                      "caption": "Saddle-shaped osteology of the CMC joint"
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "abduction-adduction motion",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [
                    {
                      "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6036/images/abduction_saddle_shape.jpg",
                      "caption": "Depiction of the abduction-adduction movement of the CMC joint"
                    }
                  ],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Muscles",
          "table": null,
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6036/images/bennet%20fx%20deforming%20forces%202.jpg",
              "caption": "Bennett"
            }
          ],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "three muscles provide deforming forces at the base of the thumb",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "abductor pollicis longus (PIN)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**proximal, dorsal, and radial force on the shaft fragment**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "extensor pollicis longus (PIN)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "4",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "proximal, dorsal, and radial force on the shaft fragment",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "1",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "text": "adductor pollicis (Ulnar n.)",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "text": "**supination and adduction force on the shaft fragment**",
                      "table": null,
                      "images": [],
                      "importance": "5",
                      "children": []
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "**Ligament**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "5",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**volar beak ligament**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/6036/images/volar_beak_ligament.jpg",
                  "caption": "Ligaments of the CMC joint"
                }
              ],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "spans the tuberosity of the trapezium to the volar edge of the 1st metacarpal",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                },
                {
                  "text": "**keeps trapezium connected to the volar-ulnar base fragment**",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "5",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "dorsoradial ligament",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "spans the dorsoradial tubercle of the trapezium to the dorsal base of the 1st metacarpal",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "2",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Biomechanics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "very limited axial rotation",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "average flexion-extension of 53 degrees",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "average abduction-adduction of 42 degrees",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
